course: dictionary
lesson: 11766
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words around the word, tells you which meaning the writer chose. «The soldiers lived in the barracks by the river.» Soldiers live in a building, so barracks means a large building where soldiers live.
explain: «The crowd began to barrack the referee after his decision.» People at a match do not build houses, so barrack here must be a verb. The clue is the crowd and the referee. Shouting rude or mocking things at someone fits the sentence, so that is the meaning to choose.
explain: In Australia and New Zealand, people say, «Who do you barrack for?» This is not about soldiers or shouting. It means «Which team do you support?» Always test your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found it.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Barracks» means a large building or group of buildings where soldiers live.
fact: In British English, «to barrack» means to shout rude or mocking things at someone, such as a player or referee.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, «to barrack for» a team means to cheer for and support it.
fact: The plural «barracks» can mean one building or many buildings.
exercise: «The soldiers lived in the barracks beside the river.» What are barracks? | Shops by the river | Tall towers built for birds | Small boats used for fishing | *Large buildings where soldiers live
exercise: «The fans began to barrack the referee after his decision.» What does barrack mean here? | To cheer and clap for the referee | *To shout rude or mocking things at someone | To sleep in a tent | To build a wall around a field
exercise: «The crowd began to barrack the player.» What is the crowd doing? | Giving the player a gift | Building a new stadium | *Shouting rude or mocking things at the player | Sleeping in the barracks
test: «The soldiers marched back to their barracks at dusk.» What are barracks in this sentence? | A small river boat | A loud shout from a crowd | A type of football boot | *A large building or group of buildings where soldiers live
test: An Australian says, «I barrack for the local footy team.» What does this mean? | I shout at the players | I play for the team | I clean the stadium after matches | *I cheer for and support the team
exam: Ben's dad says, «Don't barrack the actor, he is only pretending!» What does barrack mean here? | To stay in a building where soldiers live | *To shout rude or mocking things at someone | To cheer and support someone | To write a letter to someone
